// The SOC Prime Active Threats. Rule references are Sigma rule IDs.
let $active_threats = [
  {
    id: "-1F8kZ0BFsA9Tj2-Amec",
    name: "Hospitals, Local Government Bodies and FPV Operators - Focus of Cyber Threat Cluster UAC-0247",
    created: 2026-04-15T14:05:40Z,
    updated: 2026-10-09T09:25:15Z,
    summary: "During March‑April 2026 CERT‑UA observed an increase in attacks targeting Ukrainian hospitals, municipal authorities and FPV operator groups. The campaigns use phishing emails with malicious HTA/HTML links that deliver custom loaders, reverse shells (RAVENSHELL) and the C# based remote access tool AGINGFLY. Additional payloads include PowerShell backdoors (SILENTLOOP), credential stealers (CHROMELEVATOR, ZAPIXDESK) and public scanning tools (RUSTSCAN). The threat actors also employ DLL side‑loading, scheduled tasks and a range of custom command‑and‑control (C2) infrastructure.",
    severity: "Critical",
    investigation: "CERT‑UA analysed dozens of incidents, extracting IOCs such as malicious executables, scripts, scheduled tasks, IP addresses and domains. The analysis identified a two‑stage loader, a reverse TCP shell with XOR encryption and a WebSocket based C2 channel that uses a static AES‑CBC key. The investigators also documented the use of legitimate utilities (mshta.exe, powershell.exe, wscript.exe) to execute the malicious HTA files.",
    response: "Security teams should immediately block the identified malicious URLs and IPs, hunt for the listed file names and scheduled tasks on endpoints, and isolate compromised hosts. Deploy detection rules for the observed command lines and monitor for the AGINGFLY WebSocket traffic. Conduct credential reset for accounts that may have been harvested.",
    mitigation: "The report recommends disabling execution of LNK, HTA and JavaScript files from email, restricting use of mshta.exe, powershell.exe and wscript.exe, and enforcing application allow‑lists. Monitoring for the creation of suspicious scheduled tasks and for network connections to the listed C2 servers is also advised.",
    attack_flow: "```mermaid\ngraph TB\n%% Class definitions\nclassDef technique fill:#ffcc99\nclassDef tool fill:#c2c2f0\nclassDef malware fill:#ff9999\n%% Node definitions\ninitial_user_exec[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1204.001 User Execution: Malicious Link</b><br/><b>Description</b>: User clicks a malicious link that triggers payload execution.\"]\nclass initial_user_exec technique\n\nsystem_binary_mshta[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1218.005 System Binary Proxy Execution: Mshta</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Use of mshta.exe to execute malicious code.\"]\nclass system_binary_mshta technique\n\npowershell_interpreter[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1059.001 PowerShell</b><br/><b>Description</b>: PowerShell used for command and scripting execution.\"]\nclass powershell_interpreter technique\n\ncompression_obfusc[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1027.015 Compression</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Compress files to hide malicious content.\"]\nclass compression_obfusc technique\n\nprocess_hollowing[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1055.012 Process Injection: Process Hollowing</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Replace code of a running process with malicious code.\"]\nclass process_hollowing technique\n\ndll_sideload[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1574.001 DLL Side‑loading</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Load malicious DLLs via side‑loading.\"]\nclass dll_sideload technique\n\nappinit_dll[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1546.010 AppInit DLLs</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Register malicious DLLs in AppInit_DLLs key for execution.\"]\nclass appinit_dll technique\n\nagingfly_tool[\"<b>Tool</b> - <b>Name</b>: AGINGFLY<br/><b>Type</b>: Remote Access Tool\"]\nclass agingfly_tool tool\n\nwebsocket_c2[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1071.001 WebSocket C2</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Use WebSocket protocol for command and control communications.\"]\nclass websocket_c2 technique\n\nxmrig_malware[\"<b>Malware</b> - <b>Name</b>: XMRIG<br/><b>Purpose</b>: Cryptocurrency mining (compute hijacking)\"]\nclass xmrig_malware malware\n\nrustscan_tool[\"<b>Tool</b> - <b>Name</b>: Rustscan<br/><b>Function</b>: Network service discovery\"]\nclass rustscan_tool tool\n\nlateral_transfer[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Transfer tools to remote systems for lateral movement.\"]\nclass lateral_transfer technique\n\ningress_transfer[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Transfer tools/files into victim environment.\"]\nclass ingress_transfer technique\n\nchromelevet_tool[\"<b>Tool</b> - <b>Name</b>: ChromeLevetor<br/><b>Purpose</b>: Extract credentials from Chrome browsers\"]\nclass chromelevet_tool tool\n\nreflective_obfusc[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Use reflective loading and embedded payloads to hide malicious code.\"]\nclass reflective_obfusc technique\n\nscheduled_tasks[\"<b>Technique</b> - <b>T1059.003 Windows Command Shell (Scheduled Tasks)</b><br/><b>Description</b>: Execute scheduled tasks via cmd shell.\"]\nclass scheduled_tasks technique\n\n%% Connections\ninitial_user_exec -->|leads_to| system_binary_mshta\nsystem_binary_mshta -->|executes| powershell_interpreter\npowershell_interpreter -->|obfuscates| compression_obfusc\ncompression_obfusc -->|injects| process_hollowing\nprocess_hollowing -->|loads| dll_sideload\ndll_sideload -->|triggers| appinit_dll\nappinit_dll -->|installs| agingfly_tool\nagingfly_tool -->|communicates via| websocket_c2\nwebsocket_c2 -->|controls| xmrig_malware\nxmrig_malware -->|discovers network via| rustscan_tool\nrustscan_tool -->|enables| lateral_transfer\nlateral_transfer -->|facilitates| ingress_transfer\ningress_transfer -->|steals| chromelevet_tool\nchromelevet_tool -->|enables| reflective_obfusc\nreflective_obfusc -->|schedules| scheduled_tasks\n```",
